<h3>What you are reading and why</h3>
<p>The assigned text is the Introduction to <a href="https://historyofeconomicthought.mcmaster.ca/hegel/history.pdf" target="_blank" class="download-link">Hegel’s <em>Philosophy of History</em></a>, pp. 14–52 in the course PDF (Sibree translation). The Introduction proper begins mid-page 14, after a brief preface by Hegel’s son. The pages before it can be skipped.</p>
<p>This excerpt covers the three parts of the Introduction you most need to understand Hegel’s project:</p>
<p><strong>Three methods of history (pp. 14–26)</strong> — Hegel opens by distinguishing Original history (eyewitness accounts like Thucydides), Reflective history (later synthesis and analysis), and Philosophical history (his own approach). This is the most accessible section; read it carefully as an entry point into how Hegel thinks.</p>
<p><strong>Reason and Spirit in history (pp. 27–45)</strong> — The philosophical core. Hegel argues that Reason governs the world and that history is the process by which Spirit — the collective mind of humanity — comes to know itself. This section introduces the key concepts the lecture pages will unpack. It is dense; read slowly, and use the handouts alongside the text.</p>
<p><strong>World-historical individuals (pp. 45–52)</strong> — Hegel’s famous account of figures like Napoleon, who seem to embody the spirit of their age and drive history forward. This section is more concrete and makes for good discussion material.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Reading tip</p>
<p>Hegel writes in long, winding sentences that can feel impenetrable at first. The strategy that works best: read for the main claim of each paragraph before worrying about the details. Ask yourself — <em>what is he asserting here?</em> The lecture pages will help you verify whether your reading is on track.</p>
</div></div>
